## Automatic Interface Implementation

```xiom
#[derive(Clone, Eq, Hash, Debug, Display)]
pub type User = {
  id: Int;
  name: Str;
  email: Str;
}
```

This single `#[derive(...)]` line generates ~100 lines of interface implementations — all fields are Clone'd, Eq'll compare each field, Hash includes each field, Debug shows field names and values, Display formats the type.

### What Can Be Derived

| Derive | Requires | Generates |
|--------|----------|-----------|
| Clone | All fields: Clone | .clone() deep copy |
| Eq | All fields: Eq | ==, != operators |
| Ord | All fields: Ord | <, >, <=, >=, .cmp() |
| Hash | All fields: Hash | .hash() for HashMap keys |
| Debug | All fields: Debug | Developer output |
| Display | All fields: Display | User-facing output |
| Default | All fields: Default | Default constructor |
| Drop | No constraints | Cleanup destructor |
| From | Single field | Type conversion |

### Derive on Enums

```xiom
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Eq)]
pub enum Status {
  Active,
  Inactive,
  Suspended(reason: Str),
}
```

### Conditional Derivation

Derive only works when ALL fields implement the interface:

```xiom
pub type MySocket = Socket;  // Socket doesn't implement Clone

// This works:
#[derive(Debug, Eq)]
pub type Connection = {
  id: Int;        // Has Debug, Eq
  active: Bool;   // Has Debug, Eq
  socket: Socket; // Has Debug only — so no Clone, Eq derive
}
```

### Derive + Manual Override

You can derive MOST interfaces and manually implement the rest:

```xiom
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Eq)]
pub type Person = {
  name: Str;
  password_hash: Str;
}

// Manual Display to hide password
impl Display for Person {
  fn fmt(self: &Person, f: &mut Formatter) -> Result[(), FmtError] {
    return f.write_fmt("Person({name})", name = self.name);
  }
}
```

